STORY RULES (very important):
- NEVER say a topic label like "खेल में", "स्वास्थ्य:", "राजनीति में", "रक्षा मोर्चे पर". Just say the news.
- Each story = 13–17 Hindi words in TWO short sentences so the viewer understands it fully:
  part 1 = WHAT happened (who, where, what); part 2 = the RESULT / why it matters / what happens next.
  Good: "RBI ने रेपो रेट 5.5% पर बरकरार रखा। यानी आपकी होम लोन EMI अभी नहीं बदलेगी।"
  Good: "पीएम मोदी ने बिहार में 12 हज़ार करोड़ की परियोजनाएं शुरू कीं। इनसे सड़क और बिजली सुधरेगी।"
  Bad (meaningless): "शेयर बाज़ार में हलचल।"  Bad (label): "खेल में, भारत जीता।"
- Split each story into exactly 2 "parts" (sometimes 3) so the picture changes with the words.
  Each part = 5–9 spoken words, and its picture must show EXACTLY what THAT part says.
  THE PICTURE TEST (this is the channel's #1 quality rule — a photo of the wrong thing is worse than no photo):
  read your "say" line, then name the ONE object/person/place in it. "visual" must describe that same thing.
  If the line says "मंदिर" the picture is a temple — never a church, a generic city or a smiling stock model.
  * Each part has:
      "say":    the spoken words (Devanagari; ISRO, RBI, IPL, GDP in English letters OK; numbers as spoken)
      "visual": one English sentence naming the ONE main subject of THIS "say" line and its action/setting
                (e.g. "gold jewellery and coins on a shop counter, price ticker behind"), Indian setting for Indian news
      "entity": exact English Wikipedia title ONLY for a real specific person/organisation/place/vehicle
                ("Narendra Modi", "Reserve Bank of India", "Donald Trump", "Vande Bharat Express"), else ""
      "search": 2–4 English stock-photo words taken FROM the "say" line's main noun
                ("gold coins jewellery" for a gold-price line, "indian railway station crowd" for a rail line)
      "source": "entity" | "stock" | "ai"
  Never reuse the same "search" for two different stories; never leave "visual" or "search" empty.
- "on_screen_text": 2–4 Hindi words headline chip for the story.
- "src": the candidate number [n].

ALSO WRITE:
- "hook": 6–10 Hindi words that stop the scroll in 2 seconds: the single BIGGEST story of the day as a punchy
